  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Not as warm with a few scattered showers possible \ntoday. \n\nA cold front will continue to move southeast and away from the area \nthis morning. Post-frontal WNW flow and DPVA with the associated mid \nlevel trough may support a few scattered showers today, mainly \nacross the North Country and the Southern Tier where somewhat deeper \nmoisture is available. Some patchy fog and drizzle will also \ncontinue today across the North Country, especially across the \nhigher terrain of the Tug Hill Plateau and western Adirondacks in \nupslope flow. Otherwise, expect a wealth of low stratus in gradually \nveering northwest flow with a few odd breaks of sun possible later \nthis afternoon. Temperatures will be much cooler than yesterday, but \nstill a few degrees above average for mid October.\n\nDry weather will prevail tonight through Thursday as high pressure \nmoves from the upper Great Lakes to southern Quebec and New England. \nNorth/northeast flow will continue to support areas of low stratus \ntonight through Thursday morning before increasing sunshine during \nthe day Thursday.\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread rain Thursday night through Friday. \n\nThe pattern will turn more active again Thursday night through \nFriday, with a period of widespread rain expected across the entire \nregion. A progressive mid level low over Colorado early Thursday \nwill phase with a strong northern stream trough over the Upper \nMidwest Thursday night, with associated strong mid/upper level \nforcing supporting a deepening surface low passing over the eastern \nGreat Lakes Thursday night through early Friday. Strong forcing and \nmoisture transport with this system will support rain overspreading \nthe region from southwest to northeast Thursday night, with periods \nof rain continuing Friday through Friday evening. \n\nRainfall amounts of 0.50\" to 1.0\" are likely with this system. It \nhas been quite dry across much of the region over the past month, so \nthis will be beneficial rain. There may be just enough instability \nto support a few embedded thunderstorms as well, especially if the \nsurface low tracks just north of the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Much colder Friday night through Saturday night with \na few wet snowflakes possible.\n\nThe surface low will continue to deepen as it moves down the Saint \nLawrence Valley Friday night, and then into the Canadian Maritimes \nSaturday. Strong cold advection within the sharp mid level trough \nwill deliver the coldest airmass of the fall season thus far by late \nFriday night and Saturday. DPVA/height falls from the mid level \ntrough and increasing lake induced instability will support the \ncontinuation of showers Friday night. \n\nThe boundary layer may become cold enough to support a few wet \nsnowflakes by late Friday night and Saturday morning. Despite strong \nlake induced instability, short fetch northwest flow and a rapidly \ndrying airmass will keep the lake response muted and brief. In terms \nof the potential for the first wet snowflakes of fall, it will be a \nrace between the drying airmass and arrival of sufficiently cold \nair. There is a low chance of a very minor coating of slush on the \ngrass across high terrain Saturday morning, otherwise no \naccumulation and zero impact is expected. Any rain/snow showers will \nend by midday Saturday with ongoing drying and subsidence. \n\nThe other story will be the much cooler temperatures, with lows \ndropping into the 30s Friday night and only recovering to the 40s \nfor highs Saturday. It will be blustery as well, with wind gusts in \nthe 25-35 mph range Friday night through Saturday, and up to 40 mph \nalong the lakeshores. Low temperatures will drop into the 30s again \nSaturday night, although winds may never become light enough to \nallow for frost formation. \n\nThe cooldown will be very brief, with above average temperatures \nquickly returning early next week.",
